      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">A bigger payload is not free in three
        currencies. Money, because every resident token is re-sent on every
        turn. Attention, because a model that reads sixty facts weights each
        one less than a model that reads six. Trust, because a fact carries no
        date.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">The plate holds no count; the counts stay in
        the prose. It does not show what a stale fact costs. That argument
        stays in the paragraph below, because a shape cannot carry it.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Trust is the third cost. A session cannot tell a fact with no date apart
      from a fact that used to be true. A stale instruction still reads as an
      instruction.
    </p>

    <p>
      A paper I read this month gave me the criteria for the audit. It is
      <em>Context Engineering: From Prompts to Corporate Multi-Agent
      Architecture</em> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09619">arXiv:2603.09619</a>).
      It states five quality criteria for an agent&rsquo;s context:
      <strong>relevance, sufficiency, isolation, economy, and provenance</strong>.
      It also frames context as the agent&rsquo;s operating system.
    </p>

    <h2>What was already there</h2>

    <p>
      Four structures already existed, and all four predate those words. I
      describe them in the paper&rsquo;s terms after the fact.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Economy, traded against sufficiency</p>
      <h3>A resident index, with bodies on demand</h3>
      <p>
        Only the index is resident. Each line is a pointer. It carries a name
        and one clause of what that memory settles. Where a trigger phrase is
        load-bearing, the line carries that too: &ldquo;read before any font
        subsetting&rdquo;, &ldquo;never re-queue this&rdquo;. The body is a
        separate file, and it loads when the pointer fires.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The tradeoff:</strong> a session does not know the fact. It
        knows that the fact exists, and where to get it. That is strictly worse
        for sufficiency and much better for economy. It is the right side of
        the trade only as long as the pointers are good enough to fire. A
        pointer that does not trigger is a fact I paid to store and never read.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ca3-pointer. One figure for two cards.

