        shape.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Provenance, at the rule layer</p>
      <h3>Dated rules, each with its founding case</h3>
      <p>
        A rule in my setup is not a bare claim. It is a claim, plus the
        incident that produced it, plus a date.
      </p>
      <p>
        One rule states that a finding becomes a deferred prompt, never a
        mid-session question. Its founding case runs like this. A scheduled
        audit finished in one paragraph. Then a stale lock file, noticed in
        passing, became a three-hour project across three repositories, with
        two pull requests and a postmortem. I consented to every step. Every
        consent came at the moment I was least able to refuse.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>Why the case travels with the rule:</strong> a bare rule
        invites an agent to reason about whether it applies. A rule with its
        founding case hands over the shape of the failure. That shape is what
        the recognition of the next instance actually takes. The case also
        keeps the rule falsifiable by me. If I cannot remember why a constraint
        exists, I cannot tell whether it expired.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Isolation</p>
      <h3>Deferred work is a self-contained prompt, not a conversation</h3>
      <p>
        When a session finds a real problem outside its scope, it does not
        raise it. It files a prompt for a fresh session. That prompt must lead
        with its <strong>blast radius</strong>: the repository, and the files
        it expects to touch. It must close with an <strong>origin line</strong>:
        the date I filed it, the repository at a specific commit, and the
        evidence.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>What each half buys:</strong> the blast radius lets the
        <em>next</em> session check for a duplicate before it files another
        one. Two queued sessions therefore cannot collide silently on the same
        file. The origin line is provenance for work picked up cold, weeks
        later, and possibly on another machine. A deferred task whose evidence
        went stale should be discoverable as stale, and not re-executed on
        faith.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">The same shape, one level up</p>
      <h3>Skills: description resident, body on demand</h3>
      <p>
        A skill is a packaged procedure. Only its one-line description sits in
        context. The procedure loads when the description matches the work. It
        is the memory-index trade above, repeated at the level of behaviour
        rather than fact. That is why a skill holds the reference material that
        a session needs only to reason <em>about</em> a rule, while the rule
        itself stays resident. The figure above shows the same trade: a pointer
        that does not trigger is a fact I paid to store and never read.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>The audit, and the three it failed</h2>

    <p>
      I scored each surface against all five criteria. Isolation and sufficiency
      held. The other three did not.
    </p>

    <table class="metrics">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Criterion</th>
          <th scope="col">Verdict</th>
          <th scope="col">The finding</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Relevance</th>
          <td>Failed</td>
          <td>Every rule file loads into every session, unconditionally</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Sufficiency</th>
          <td>Held</td>
          <td>Pointers fired; bodies carried the detail</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Isolation</th>
          <td>Held</td>
          <td>Deferred prompts stand alone; one concern per session</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Economy</th>
          <td>Failed</td>
          <td>Three index lines grew into changelogs. The worst ran to roughly 230 words</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Provenance</th>
          <td>Failed</td>
          <td>Not one index line carried a date. The failure was total</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      <strong>Provenance was the worst of the three.</strong> No index line
      said when I wrote it, or when I last touched it. So a session that reads
      the index cannot tell yesterday&rsquo;s fact from a June fact that July
      quietly superseded. Every line makes its claim with exactly the same
      confidence.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Economy failed in a way that fed the first.</strong> The
      file&rsquo;s whole job is to hold pointers. Those three lines pointed at
      nothing, and carried the body file&rsquo;s own detail instead. An index
      line that reads like a changelog is no longer a pointer. It is the shape
      most likely to go stale, because an append to it is easier than an edit
      to the body.
    </p>

    <!-- The relevance paragraph below is left byte-exact on purpose. It is
         argument end to end, and its closing claim is the one the whole
         section turns on: a rule that fails to load looks exactly like a rule
         that was followed. The redesign brief rules it untouched, and a
         reworded qualifier is where the strength of an argument leaks. -->
    <p>
      <strong>Relevance failed too, and I did not fix it.</strong> None of the
      rule files are path-scoped, so a session renaming a CSS token still reads
      the multi-vendor routing doctrine in full. I left it alone because the
      alternative failure is silent and worse: scope a rule to a path, guess the
      path wrong, and the session that most needed the rule is the one that never
      loaded it. A rule that fails to load looks exactly like a rule that was
      followed. I would rather pay tokens than take that trade before I can
      measure it.
    </p>

    <h2>What shipped this week</h2>

    <p>Three conventions and one hook.</p>

    <p>
      Every index line now ends with a compact date stamp. Where no date is
      determinable, the line says so and never guesses. A cap holds every line
      to 25 words. Displaced text merges down into the body file, and I delete
      none of it. The cap carries one explicit carve-out:
      <strong>compression removes detail, never a verdict and never a
      trigger.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      &ldquo;Read before touching X&rdquo; is the load-bearing part of a line,
      and a shorter line that dropped it is worse than the long one. Deferred
      prompts now close with the origin line above.
    </p>

    <p>
      All three of those are prose. The fourth is a hook.
    </p>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>A prose rule promoted to a mechanism</p>
      <h3>The guard that covered the surface its author enumerated</h3>
      <p>
        One of my oldest rules is that an agent stages explicit paths and never
        runs <code>git add -A</code>. Parallel sessions leave dirty working
        trees, and a wildcard stage scoops up another session&rsquo;s in-flight
        work. Prose is a weak place for that rule to live. So I promoted it to a
        hook before this audit, and the hook inspects the command before it
        runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        I audited the hook and found two commands that walked straight past it.
        Both stage the entire tree. I drove both through the guard on standard
        input, and the guard allowed both.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>git stage -A</code>. <code>stage</code> is a built-in synonym for <code>add</code>, and it takes the same options to the same effect. The matcher matched only the spelling <code>add</code>.</li>
        <li><code>git add -Au</code>. This is a valid combined short flag. The matcher compared whole tokens. It read <code>-Au</code> as an unfamiliar flag, and it passed the command through.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- ca2-bypass. The gap is the claim, so the gap is the geometry.

           The add branch is an outline with two segments left out of its
           bottom edge, and the two commands leave through them. The commit
           branch is closed on all four sides, because it already scanned a
           flag one letter at a time. Both branches sit inside one guard.

           Oxide marks the two commands and their edges only. The card around
           this plate already carries data-outcome="reversal", so oxide is the
           page's own marking rather than a new one. The branch outlines stay
           ink: a branch with a gap is not a reversal.

           No digit (ADR-013). The test-suite counts stay in the prose below.
           The words carry every state, because Geist has no glyph for a cross
           or a tick and this site refuses to add one.

           Plate 03 of three, in reading order. This figure takes NO
           data-outcome="reversal" of its own. The .decision card around it
           already carries that attribute, its doubled oxide mark, and the
           sr-only "Reversed. " prefix. A figure-level mark would state one
           reversal twice and announce it twice. The figure sits inside a
           .decision, so it keeps the entry's measure and is not promoted
           (ADR-014, A5); data-place is therefore declarative here. -->
      <figure class="ca2-fig fig" data-fig="plate" data-place="spine">
        <span class="fig-label">Plate 03</span>
        <svg class="ca2-plate ca-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 132" role="img"
             aria-label="One guard, two branches. The commit branch scans a flag letter by letter. The add branch compares whole tokens. Two commands walked out through the gap: git stage -A and git add -Au.">
          <title>Two branches of one guard. Only one of them scans a flag letter by letter.</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="ca2-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                    markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
              <path class="ca-head-out" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <text class="ca-lane" x="2" y="10">ONE GUARD, TWO BRANCHES</text>

          <rect class="ca-box" x="2" y="16" width="256" height="36" rx="2" />
          <text class="ca-title" x="10" y="31">commit branch</text>
          <text class="ca-micro" x="10" y="45">scans a flag letter by letter</text>

          <path class="ca-open" d="M 54 96 L 4 96 A 2 2 0 0 1 2 94 L 2 60 A 2 2 0 0 1 4 58 L 256 58 A 2 2 0 0 1 258 60 L 258 94 A 2 2 0 0 1 256 96 L 176 96 M 66 96 L 164 96" aria-hidden="true" />
          <text class="ca-title" x="10" y="73">add branch</text>
          <text class="ca-micro" x="10" y="87">compares whole tokens</text>

          <path class="ca-edge-out" d="M 60 96 L 60 106" marker-end="url(#ca2-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
          <path class="ca-edge-out" d="M 170 96 L 170 106" marker-end="url(#ca2-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
          <text class="ca-cmd" x="60" y="118" text-anchor="middle">git stage -A</text>
          <text class="ca-micro" x="60" y="129" text-anchor="middle">a synonym</text>
          <text class="ca-cmd" x="170" y="118" text-anchor="middle">git add -Au</text>
          <text class="ca-micro" x="170" y="129" text-anchor="middle">one whole token</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-what">The same guard has two branches, and only one
          of them scans a flag letter by letter. The add branch compares whole
          tokens, so two commands walked out through the gap. I wrote both
          branches on the same day.</span>
          <span class="fig-limit">The plate shows the gap, not the fix: not the
          letter match that closed the two bypasses, and not the near-miss
          cases that bound it. Both stay in the prose below. The plate also
          holds no count, so the test-suite figures stay there too.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The sharp part:</strong> the same guard&rsquo;s commit branch
        <em>already</em> solved the combined-flag problem. It scans the letters
        of a flag like <code>-am</code> one at a time. The second branch never
        got the treatment the first one got. A matcher covers the surface its
        author enumerated, not the surface that exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fix matches the operation rather than the spelling. The widening is
        bounded rather than guessed: no other <code>git add</code> short option
        uses those letters, so a letter match cannot collide. The test suite
        went from 24 cases to 32. I split the added cases deliberately between
        the two closed bypasses and the near misses that prove the fix did not
        widen too far. <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops/pull/102">The change is public</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      A mechanism that cannot be persuaded now enforces the staging rule. The
      three conventions that shipped alongside it have no such mechanism.
      <strong>Nothing fails if a date stamp is wrong, or absent, or
      invented.</strong> The context is legible. It is not yet checkable.
    </p>

    <h2>The same gaps in production surveys</h2>

    <p>
      LangChain&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.langchain.com/state-of-agent-engineering">State
      of Agent Engineering</a> survey puts numbers on where teams that run
      agents in production are actually stuck. <strong>32% name quality</strong>
      as their top barrier: accuracy, consistency, and hallucination. Among
      organisations over 2,000 people, <strong>security is second at
      24.9%</strong>. The adoption gap in the same report is the one I recognise
      most. Around 89% have implemented observability, and 52% have implemented
      evaluations.
    </p>

    <p>
      Those figures map onto this architecture. <strong>Quality</strong> is what
      provenance and economy are for. An agent that acts on a fact that expired
      in July produces a confident, well-formed, wrong answer. No amount of
      model capability fixes an input that is no longer true.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Security</strong> is what isolation is for. I enforce the redlines
      in my setup per vendor, mechanically, at tool-call time. A constraint
      written in a document is a request, and five vendors with different
      harnesses will honour a request differently.
    </p>

    <p>
      The observability-versus-evaluation gap is the same weakness in my own
      work. I can read my context. I cannot yet fail a build over it.
    </p>

